[Line "9.0-0-0 d5: main line with 12.Bd4 Bxd4"]
[Section "9.0-0-0 d5"]
[Summary "The main line of the modern Dragon. Black trades bishops, gains time on the queen and lives with split queenside pawns; with exact play he wins the h-pawn and reaches a balanced ending."]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 g6 6. Be3 Bg7 7. f3 O-O 8. Qd2 Nc6 9. O-O-O { [%cal
Gd6d5] White saves the three bishop moves of the 9.Bc4 lines and castles at once. The price is that he
no longer controls d5. } 9... d5 { The principled reply, and a pawn sacrifice. Jones's rule is that when
the Dragon player can play ...d5 he should: the break frees the position and gets rid of the hole on d5.
The alternatives 9...Nxd4 10.Bxd4 Be6 and 9...Bd7 are playable, but they give White the attacking set-up
he wants without the tempi Bc4 costs. } 10. exd5 { The tenth-move alternatives 10.Qe1, 10.Kb1 and
10.Nxc6 have their own lines. } 10... Nxd5 11. Nxc6 bxc6 { [%cal Ga8b8,Gd8b6] [%csl Yc5,Rb2] The
structure of the whole variation. Black has split pawns and a hole on c5; in return the b-file is open
onto b2 and the bishop on g7 looks through to c3. } 12. Bd4 { [%cal Rc3e4,Re4c5] White declines the pawn
(12.Nxd5 is a separate line) and offers to trade the bishop that makes the b-file dangerous. Once it is
gone he hopes to settle a knight on c5. } 12... Bxd4 { Jones's choice. Black gives up the bishop, but
gains a tempo on the queen and keeps a sound centre: no pawn on e5 means no hole on d6 and an open
diagonal for the queen. The older 12...e5 has its own line; 12...Nxc3 13.Qxc3 Bh6+ is a third way that
has scored less well. } 13. Qxd4 Qb6 { [%cal Gb6d4] Offering an exchange that White cannot accept: 14.Qxb6
axb6 would repair Black's pawns and open the a-file. } 14. Na4 { With tempo, and on the way to c5. }
( 14. Nxd5 cxd5 15. Qxb6 axb6 { Black's pawns are healthy again and the a-file is open. The ending is
equal and Black has scored well in it. } )
14... Qc7 { The retreat most strong players choose. 14...Qa5 first, to provoke 15.b3, is the alternative
Jones also covers: it loosens the dark squares round the king, but it gives the king air as well, and
White's results after 15.b3 have been better. } 15. Bc4 { Pinning nothing yet, but lining up on d5 and
f7. 15.Nc5 Rd8 16.Bc4 comes to the same thing. } 15... Rd8 { [%cal Gd8d4] The rook faces the queen, so
that any knight move will come with tempo. } 16. Nc5 { [%csl Yc5] The outpost. From here the knight
covers b7, d7 and e6 and shuts the c-file. }
( 16. h4 { Too slow. } 16... Bf5 { Black is fully developed and attacks c2, with ...Nb6 or ...Nb4 to
follow; White has done badly here. } )
16... Bf5 { [%cal Gf5c2] Development with a threat. } 17. Bb3 { Covering c2 and stepping out of the way of
...Nb6. } 17... Nb6 { [%cal Gd8d4,Gc7h2] The accurate move. The knight uncovers the rook against the
queen, and when the rooks come off the h2-pawn falls. The natural 17...Nf4 18.Qf2 has scored badly:
after the exchange on d1 White keeps all his pressure and Black has won nothing. } 18. Qe3 Rxd1+ 19. Rxd1
Qxh2 { Black has won a pawn. In return White gets a tempo on the bishop and the only open file. } 20. g4
Nd5 { An in-between move that saves the bishop: the queen is attacked first. } 21. Qd2 Qxd2+ 22. Kxd2 Bc8
23. Ba4 Rb8 { **Verdict:** balanced. Black is a pawn up, White has a knight on c5, pressure on c6 and
the more active king, and the elite games from here have divided evenly. Black's plan is to unravel with
...Kg7, ...Nb6 or ...Nf4 and ...h5, creating a passed pawn; White tries to win c6 or a7 first. } *

[Line "9.0-0-0 d5: the old main line 12.Bd4 e5"]
[Section "9.0-0-0 d5"]
[Summary "Black keeps the bishops and offers the exchange on f8. The most played line in practice and the one Giri builds on with 13...Re8; White's pressure is lasting but manageable."]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 g6 6. Be3 Bg7 7. f3 O-O 8. Qd2 Nc6 9. O-O-O d5 10. exd5
Nxd5 11. Nxc6 bxc6 12. Bd4 e5 { [%csl Rd6] The traditional move, still the most common one in practice.
Black keeps the bishop and gains space with tempo. The cost is the long diagonal, which is closed until
...e4 can be played, and a hole on d6 for White's knight. } 13. Bc5 { [%cal Rc5f8] } 13... Be6 { [%cal
Gg7h6,Ga8b8] The move that made the line: Black leaves the rook to be taken. }
( 13... Re8 { Giri's recommendation, avoiding the offer. His book treats 14.Nxd5, 14.Bc4 and 14.Ne4 as
the main replies. After 14.Nxd5 cxd5 15.Qxd5 Qxd5 16.Rxd5 Be6 Black is a pawn down in an ending that Ward
judges tenable, with the bishop pair and two open files. Against the main move, the independent idea is
to keep the bishop on c8 and play the queen and the f-pawn. } 14. Ne4 Qc7 15. h4 f5 16. Nd6 Rd8 17. Nxc8
Raxc8 { White has the bishop pair and Black a strong centre and all his pieces in play; the results from
here are close to level. } )
14. Ne4 { [%cal Re4d6,Re4g5] White declines and brings the knight to the centre. }
( 14. Bxf8? { Taking the exchange gives up the dark squares for good. } 14... Qxf8 { Black threatens
...Bh6, pinning the queen, and follows with ...Rb8 and ...Qb4 against b2 while the bishop on g7 comes to
life with ...e4. This is the standard example of the Dragon bishop being worth more than a rook. } )
14... Re8 { The rook steps out of the attack. 14...Rb8 is the sharper try, still offering the exchange;
the old textbooks answer it with 15.Bc4. } 15. h4 { [%cal Rh4h5] The critical move, according to Ward:
White wants h5 before Black is organised. } 15... h5 { Stopping the pawn where it stands, and
keeping g5 and g4 under control so that the knight can use f4. }
( 15... h6 { The classical main line, meeting h5 with ...g5. After 16.g4 Qc7 17.g5 h5 18.Bc4 Red8 Black's
position holds, but White's bind on the dark squares lasts, and this is the line Jones had in mind when
he wrote that Black was suffering. } )
16. Bc4 Nf4 { [%cal Gd8d2] Forcing matters: the bishop on e6 is attacked, and the queens come off. }
17. Bxe6 Qxd2+ 18. Rxd2 Nxe6 19. Be3 f5 { **Verdict:** a playable ending. Black has driven the bishop back
and gains space with ...f5 and ...e4, or ...f4; the knight is as good as the bishop, and the weak pawns
on c6 and a7 are hard to attack. White keeps a symbolic edge because of the structure. Practice is
close to level, and it is an easier position to handle than the middlegames with 15...h6. } *

[Line "9.0-0-0 d5: the pawn grab 12.Nxd5"]
[Section "9.0-0-0 d5"]
[Summary "White takes the pawn. Black replies 13...Qc7, offering both rooks for the queen, and gets open files against the king whatever White does."]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 g6 6. Be3 Bg7 7. f3 O-O 8. Qd2 Nc6 9. O-O-O d5 10. exd5
Nxd5 11. Nxc6 bxc6 12. Nxd5 cxd5 13. Qxd5 { White has won a pawn and attacks the rook on a8. } 13... Qc7 {
[%cal Gc8f5,Ga8b8,Gg7b2] The move that makes 9...d5 possible. Black ignores the rook: with the b- and
c-files open and both bishops aimed at the king, development matters more than material. Jones's verdict
is that taking the material, whether the pawn alone or the two rooks as well, is dangerous for White. }
14. Qc5 { White returns to the defence and offers the queens. }
( 14. Qxa8 { Taking everything. } 14... Bf5 { [%cal Gc7c2,Gf8a8] Mate is threatened on c2 and the queen
is attacked, so it has to give itself for the second rook. } 15. Qxf8+ Kxf8 16. Rd2 { White has two rooks and a pawn for the queen but
is behind in development. }
( 16. Bd3? { A common mistake. } 16... Qe5 { [%cal Ge5e3,Ge5b2] A double attack on e3 and b2 that wins
material. } )
16... h5 { Black needs this move at some point, to give the king air and to hold up g4. The queen and
bishops then go after the queenside pawns. Black has full compensation, as Hansen puts it, and in elite
games he has scored heavily; the only question is whether it can be won. } )
14... Qb7 { [%cal Gb7b2] Keeping the queens on and hitting b2. } 15. Qa3 { [%cal Ra3e7] The queen defends
b2 and a2 from the side and eyes e7. }
( 15. Qb5 { An offer to repeat. } 15... Qc7 { After 16.Qc5 Qb7 the position repeats, and a draw this way
is a common end to the line. A player who wants more can exchange on b5 and play the ending a pawn down
with the bishops and both files, which has scored well. } )
( 15. b3 { Blocking the b-file at the cost of the long diagonal. } 15... Bf5 16. Bd3 Rac8 { [%cal
Gc8c5,Gf5d3] Gaining a tempo on the queen. All Black's pieces are aimed at c2 and c3, and the pawn on b3
has left the dark squares round the king without a defender. } )
15... Bf5 { [%cal Gf5c2,Ga8b8,Gf8c8] } 16. Bd3 { Challenging the bishop that attacks c2. } 16... Rab8 17. b3
{ **Verdict:** Black has lasting compensation: both rooks have files, the bishop on g7 has the long
diagonal to itself and the white king will never be comfortable. But care is needed here. The routine
17...Rfc8 18.Bxf5 gxf5 has not done well in elite games, because White gets his rook to d3 and
consolidates. This is the one position of the 9.0-0-0 complex to prepare from Jones's fourth chapter
before relying on it; the repetition with 15.Qb5 and the ending after 14.Qxa8 hold no dangers. } *

[Line "9.0-0-0 d5: 10.Qe1"]
[Section "9.0-0-0 d5"]
[Summary "The queen leaves the d-file so that the rook attacks d5. Black answers 10...e5 and reaches a version of the old main line."]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 g6 6. Be3 Bg7 7. f3 O-O 8. Qd2 Nc6 9. O-O-O d5 10. Qe1 {
[%cal Rd1d5] An odd-looking move with a clear point: the rook now attacks d5 directly, and the queen
watches e4 and e5 from behind. It avoids the heavily analysed lines with 12.Bd4 Bxd4. } 10... e5 { Jones's answer, and by far the usual one. Black holds d5 by attacking the
knight. The solid alternative is 10...e6. } 11. Nxc6 bxc6 12. exd5 Nxd5 { The knight must recapture.
After 12...cxd5 13.Bg5 the bishop pins the knight and uncovers the queen against e5, which is the
tactical point of putting the queen on e1. } 13. Bc4 Be6 14. Ne4 { [%cal Re4c5,Re3c5] The same plan as in
the old main line: bishop to c5, knight to c5 or d6. }
( 14. Kb1 { The fashionable move among strong players, waiting to see Black's set-up. } 14... Rb8 { A
useful move in return. 14...Re8 is the other standard reply; what Black must not play is 14...Qc7,
because 15.Nxd5 cxd5 16.Bxd5 wins a pawn. } )
14... Qc7 15. Bc5 Rfd8 16. h4 h6 { [%cal Gd5f4] Preparing to answer h5 with ...g5, as in the old main
line. After 17.g4 Nf4 Black exchanges the light-squared bishops and has a firm grip on f4.

**Verdict:** White is a little better. This is the old 12.Bd4 e5 structure with the white queen on e1,
where it is safer than on d2, and White's results in practice are good. Black's position is sound and
the plans are the familiar ones (...Nf4, ...Rd7 and doubling, ...a5), but this is the line of the
9.0-0-0 complex in which Black has to defend most patiently. } *

[Line "9.0-0-0 d5: 10.Kb1"]
[Section "9.0-0-0 d5"]
[Summary "A waiting move with a tactical idea: after 10...Nxd4 White plays 11.e5. Black gets a good game by giving the exchange, or by a queen sacrifice."]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 g6 6. Be3 Bg7 7. f3 O-O 8. Qd2 Nc6 9. O-O-O d5 10. Kb1 {
[%csl Yb1] The king move improves every version of the pawn grab on d5, and it sets a trap. }
10... Nxd4 { The right reply, and the only good one. }
( 10... e5?! { Playing as against 10.Qe1 is what White hopes for. } 11. Nxc6 bxc6 12. exd5 Nxd5 13. Nxd5
cxd5 14. Qxd5 { This is the pawn grab of the 12.Nxd5 line with Kb1 thrown in and the bishop on g7 shut
in by its own e-pawn. Black has little to show for the pawn. } )
11. e5 { [%cal Re5f6] The point of 10.Kb1: both knights are attacked. }
( 11. Bxd4 dxe4 { The centre is liquidated and Black has no weaknesses, although White's results in the
few elite games have been good and accuracy is still required. } )
11... Nf5 { [%cal Gf5e3] The knight escapes with tempo on the bishop. } 12. exf6 exf6 { Giri's choice and
the usual move. Black has an extra pawn for the moment and keeps the bishop on g7. }
( 12... Bxf6 { The older line, leading to a famous queen sacrifice worked out by Golubev: after 13.Nxd5
Black takes the knight with the queen. Rook, bishop and pawn for the queen, with two fine bishops, is
thought to be just about enough, and in practice it has scored well. } 13. Nxd5 Qxd5 14. Qxd5 Nxe3 15. Qd2
Nxd1 16. Qxd1 Be6 )
13. Bc5 { Attacking the rook. }
( 13. Nxd5 { Winning the pawn back and centralising the knight. } 13... Nxe3 14. Qxe3 Be6 15. Bc4 f5 {
Opening the bishop. } 16. Bb3 Re8 { Black has the two bishops against a strong knight. The position is
about equal, but it is easier for White, who has scored well. } )
13... d4 { The exchange sacrifice Giri recommends and Carlsen has played. The rook is left to be taken
so that the knight can come to e3 with tempo. } 14. Bxf8 Qxf8 15. Nb5 Ne3 { [%csl Ye3] The knight on e3,
protected by the d-pawn, paralyses White's rooks. } 16. Re1 f5 { Opening the long diagonal. } 17. f4 Qc5 {
**Verdict:** dynamically equal. Black has a pawn, the bishop pair and the knight on e3 for the exchange,
and White has to return material to free himself, usually by taking on d4 or e3. The line is forcing
and worth learning exactly, since one slip by either side decides the game. } *

[Line "9.0-0-0 d5: 10.Nxc6 and the minor tenth moves"]
[Section "9.0-0-0 d5"]
[Summary "White takes on c6 first so that Black cannot capture on e4, then goes for Bh6 or Bc4. Black's counterplay on the b-file is fast enough; the other tenth moves are met by 10...dxe4."]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 g6 6. Be3 Bg7 7. f3 O-O 8. Qd2 Nc6 9. O-O-O d5 10. Nxc6 {
White strengthens Black's centre and opens the b-file, but with the queen pinned on the d-file ...dxe4
is not available, and he gains a move for the attack. }
( 10. h4 { Not dangerous, since Black's central play is already under way. } 10... dxe4 11. h5 Nxd4
12. Bxd4 e5 { The simplest way to stop the attack, in Jones's words. } 13. h6 exd4 14. hxg7 Re8 { The pawn
on g7 looks frightening and does nothing. } 15. Nxe4 Nxe4 16. fxe4 Qf6 { The queen comes to the defence,
and after ...Qxg7 Black is a safe pawn up; White has just enough activity to hold. } )
( 10. Bh6 { Trading bishops at the cost of the centre. } 10... Bxh6 11. Qxh6 Nxd4 12. Rxd4 e5 13. Rxd5 {
White's best chance is this exchange sacrifice. } 13... Nxd5 14. exd5 f5 { White has some compensation
and no more. 10...dxe4 is a simpler route to equality. } )
10... bxc6 11. Bh6 { The sharpest idea. 11.exd5 Nxd5 is the main line by transposition. }
( 11. Bc4 { Using the pin to bring the bishop to b3. } 11... e6 12. Bb3 Bb7 { Jones's set-up. The bishop
on b7 blocks the b-file, but the one on b3 is shut out by Black's pawns, and the game is level. } )
11... Bxh6 12. Qxh6 Rb8 { [%cal Gb8b2,Gd8a5] Both sides are now playing for mate. Hansen prefers 12...Qb6
with the same ideas; the lines run together. } 13. e5 { White's main try, driving the knight from f6. }
( 13. h4 Qa5 { [%cal Gb8b2] Jones's improvement. The rook sacrifice on b2 follows, and Black's attack
arrives first. } )
13... Nd7 14. h4
( 14. Rd4 { The rook heads for h4. } 14... Re8 15. e6 Nf6 16. exf7+ Kxf7 { A complicated position in which
Jones likes Black's central pawns. } )
14... Nxe5 15. h5 Bf5 16. g4 f6 { [%cal Gg6g5] The idea that holds everything together. The bishop cannot
be taken: 17.gxf5 g5 and the queen is trapped. } 17. Qe3 { The best square. }
( 17. Qf4? { Walking into a discovered attack. } 17... Qd6 { [%cal Gd6b4,Ge5d3] White has to lose a move
with the queen. } 18. Qe3 Qb4 { Black's attack on b2 is decisive; this is how Marin won a model game. } )
17... Bd7 18. hxg6 Qb6 { Forcing the queens off; 18...hxg6 19.f4 would be dangerous. } 19. gxh7+ Kh8
20. Qxb6 axb6 { **Verdict:** equal. White is a pawn up for a moment, but the pawn on h7 falls and Black
has the more compact structure. The lines are sharp and short: know 12...Rb8, the trick 16...f6 and the
queen exchange with 18...Qb6, and 10.Nxc6 holds no terrors. } *
